 "transcript": {
  "answer": "**The cash rate is up about 4.25 percentage points since January 2022, and Sydney rents are rising 3.5% a year (August 2026).**\n\n**Cash rate (RBA, monthly average)**\n- **January 2022:** 0.10%\n- **September 2026:** 4.36%, which is roughly the 4.35% target. The extra 0.01 is probably a small quirk of the monthly averaging, not a policy move.\n\nIt didn't rise in a straight line:\n- **May 2022 to December 2023:** a fast series of hikes took it from 0.10% to a peak of 4.35%.\n- **2024:** it stayed at 4.35% all year.\n- **February to September 2025:** cuts brought it down to 3.60%, where it stayed until January 2026.\n- **February to June 2026:** hikes took it back up to 4.35%, where it has stayed since.\n\n**Sydney rents (ABS monthly CPI, change from a year earlier)**\n- **Latest (August 2026):** 3.5%, the same as in June and July.\n- **January 2025:** 5.6%. Growth then slowed to a low of 3.2% in August–September 2025.\n- **Since then:** it has stayed between 3.3% and 3.5%.\n\nSo Sydney rents are rising much more slowly than in early 2025, but they're still going up at about 3.5% a year, and growth has picked up slightly in recent months.",
